/T (Performance Analysis)
/E (Performance Analysis)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 665 0 R
/E (Challenge with Kiwis: Performance was consistently lower for Kiwis compared to Avocados aligning with the baseline findings.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 666 0 R
/E (The Baseline Gap:   The baseline study \(Varga et al., 2021\) achieved higher scores using a CNN: 93.3% on Avocado and 66.7% on Kiwis. We did not compare directly because we used a stricter data split to avoid data leakage.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8 9]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 667 0 R
/E (The Transformer Constraint: While we achieved 82.1% accuracy on Avocados , our results confirm that Transformers are         data-hungry. The dataset size limited the model's ability to outperform lightweight CNNs.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 668 0 R
/E (Band reduction is a key design choice in the HSI setting.)
/Pg 15 0 R
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The path forward)
/E (The path forward)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 669 0 R
/E (Why HSI Transformers? Despite data limitations, the approach is promising. Transformers can capture long-range dependencies between distant spectral bands, linking visible color changes to internal chemical shifts in the NIR range.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 670 0 R
/E (Scaling Up: Future work must prioritize expanding the dataset. With sufficient data, Transformers could likely scale better than CNNs.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 671 0 R
/E (Commercial Viability: Future systems could reduce data volume by processing only specific "Regions of Interest" \(few pixels\) or specific band subsets rather than full hypercubes. Companies like OneThird are already deploying avocado scanners.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
